-- CONTROL 3/3 for s6-c1-rigid-goal-no-minimum -- and the one that makes the word
-- "rigid" an ASSERTION rather than a label.
--
-- Identical to that fixture except that `main` NEVER CALLS `firstRow`. So the
-- program poses exactly one `Index` goal, and it is the DEFINITION-SITE one:
--
--     Index (List (List a)) Int (List a)      with `a` rigid
--
-- There is no ground goal anywhere. If the reject were really keyed to groundness
-- -- if it needed the `firstRow [[1, 2], [3]]` call to instantiate `a := Int`
-- before firing -- this file would be ACCEPTED and its sibling's "rigid" would be
-- decoration on a ground-goal test.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived, NOT captured): REJECT, byte-identically to the
-- sibling. §3's precedence paragraph is explicit that `assum` stays silent here and
-- §6.1.3 that `inst`'s commitment is final, so the rigid goal is put to `min⊑`
-- and answered by it; §6.2 T3 makes it closed; §6 C1 finds two ⊑-incomparable
-- matching instances. Nothing in that derivation mentions a use site.
--
-- ⚠️ Do not "simplify" this by deleting `firstRow`'s signature. An unsignatured
-- `firstRow` generalizes the same way but the residual predicate would be
-- abstracted by §4 `gen` into a dict parameter -- C1's exit (ii) -- and the goal
-- would never reach `inst` at all. The signature with NO declared context is
-- precisely what makes the predicate residual-but-not-abstracted.
impl Index (List (List b)) k c where
  index _ _ = panic "user Index fired"

firstRow : List (List a) -> List a
firstRow xss = index xss 0

main = println "firstRow is never called"
